HEAD(1)                        P32W Unix                         HEAD(1)

NAME
       head - print the first lines of a file

SYNOPSIS
       head [-n count | -count] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       head copies the first count lines (default 10) of each file,
       or of the standard input if no file is named (or for a file
       named -), to the standard output. If the input has fewer
       lines it is copied whole. Lines may be of any length.

       With more than one file, each file's lines are preceded by a
       header line ==> name <==, and the headers after the first by
       an empty line.

OPTIONS
       -n count
       -count print count lines instead of 10, e.g. -n 20 or -20
              (also -n20). A count that is not a number is a usage
              error; 0 prints nothing.

EXAMPLES
       head /etc/passwd              the first ten lines
       head -3 notes.txt             the first three
       ls -l | head -5
       head -1 *.c                   the first line of each

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or if a file could not be opened
       (head says why and goes on with the next).

SEE ALSO
       tail(1), cat(1), more(1)

BUGS
       There is no -c (bytes), no -q or -v, and no negative count
       (all but the last lines). The standard input's header says
       standard input.
